.TH NOG 1 "October 2026" "nog v1.5.8" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
nog \- tier-aware package manager for KognogOS
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nog
.I <command>
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ packages ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nog
is the package manager for KognogOS. It wraps
.BR pacman (8)
\(em and optionally an AUR helper such as
.BR yay (8)
or
.BR paru (8)
\(em with a three-tier update management system, giving every package on the
system a protection level based on how critical it is.
.PP
Tier 1 packages (the kernel, bootloader, glibc, systemd, mesa) are held for
30 days after upstream publish before auto-releasing. Tier 2 packages are
held for 15 days. Tier 3 packages are held for 7 days. Expert users can
switch Tier 1 to a manual-sign-off mode via
.B tier-pins.toml
if they prefer to eyeball every kernel upgrade personally. See
.B TIER SYSTEM
for the full model.
.PP
.B nog
should always be run as your regular user. It escalates to root only where
genuinely required — by invoking
.B sudo pacman
for package transactions and
.B sudo tee
for writing
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml .
See the
.B PRIVILEGES AND SUDO
section below for the complete privilege model.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B nog install \fIpackage\fR [\fIpackage\fR...] | \fIfile\fR.pkg.tar.* [\fIfile\fR...]
Install one or more packages. Tier classification is printed as informational
output; installs are never blocked or gated — explicit user commands execute
user intent. If an AUR helper is configured (see
.BR nog.conf (5)),
the install routes through the helper, so AUR-only packages work transparently
without any pre-check. Tier protection applies to future updates, not to installs.
.IP
Since v1.5.8 pacman asks before installing (no \fB--noconfirm\fR), showing
exactly what will be installed, dependencies included. When the install has to
update first, nog shows only what will install, as a table, and the holds as
one line; pacman is given exactly those packages
(\fBpacman -Sy --needed\fR \fIready\fR... \fB--ignore\fR \fIheld\fR).
.IP
Since v1.5.0 a locally built package file \(em any argument containing
.B .pkg.tar
\(em is installed with
.BR "pacman -U" ,
for example the output of
.BR makepkg (8).
Its name, version and tier are read from the file itself, since a local build
has no repository entry. Names and files cannot be mixed in one command; they
are separate pacman operations, and splitting one request could leave half of
it installed. A mistyped path is reported as a missing file, never searched for
in the repositories. There is no signature gate beyond pacman's own
.BR LocalFileSigLevel .
.IP
Since v1.5.6, \fBnog install\fR first checks how long ago the package lists were
refreshed on this machine. When a list is missing or more than a day old, it runs
the tier-aware update first (ready updates install, holds stay; when nothing may
be installed, the lists are refreshed with \fBpacman -Sy\fR), then the install.
Refreshing only the lists and installing one package would be a partial upgrade,
which Arch does not support. If that update is cancelled or fails, the install
stops and says why.
.IP
Since v1.5.7 the decision also reads nog's record of its last completed update,
\fI~/.local/share/nog/last-update\fR, written only when the lists were refreshed and
the repository step finished. The update runs first when that record is missing,
more than a day old, or older than the lists: pacman refreshes the lists before it
asks to proceed, so a declined prompt, or \fBpacman -Sy\fR run by hand, leaves
fresh lists and nothing installed.
.IP
Since v1.5.4, when standard input is not a terminal and a requested name is not
in any enabled repository (so the helper would build it from the AUR), nog
refuses before handing off and says why: the helper stops to let you review the
build recipe, and nothing is at the keyboard to answer. Answering for you would
skip that review, so nog does not.
.TP
.B nog clean
Clear old package downloads from pacman's cache (every \fBCacheDir\fR in
.IR pacman.conf ).
Since v1.5.5. Retention follows the tier: Tier 1 keeps 3 versions of each
package, Tier 2 keeps 2, Tier 3 keeps 1 (\fBtier1_keep\fR, \fBtier2_keep\fR,
\fBtier3_keep\fR under \fB[clean]\fR in
.IR nog.conf ).
The installed version always counts as one and is never removed; a version
newer than the installed one (downloaded, not yet installed) is kept; every file
of a package that is no longer installed is removed; a \fB.sig\fR goes with its
package. Leftover \fBdownload-XXXXXX\fR folders older than a day are removed
too. Versions are ordered exactly as pacman orders them. nog shows what it would
remove, per tier, with sizes, and asks \fB[y/N]\fR; with no answer nothing is
removed. It refuses while pacman's lock file exists.
.TP
.B nog remove \fIpackage\fR [\fIpackage\fR...]
Remove one or more packages via
.BR pacman (8),
with what only they needed (\fB-Rs\fR). Works uniformly for packages originally
installed from official repos or AUR. Since v1.5.8 pacman shows the list and asks
before removing anything.
.TP
.B nog update
Compute a tier-aware system upgrade plan and hand each source to the tool
that owns it \(em
.BR pacman (8)
for the official repositories, then the configured AUR helper, then
.BR flatpak (1)
and
.BR snap (8).
Every pending upgrade is shown before any transaction starts. Since v1.5.4 a
.B SUMMARY
table comes first: one row per official repository in
.I pacman.conf
order, then AUR, Flatpak and Snap, each with its Ready, Held and total counts
(plus an \fBAsk you\fR column when anything is Unknown). A source whose query
failed reads \fBcould not check\fR, never 0; one switched off with
.B nog deactivate
reads \fBoff\fR; one that is not installed has no row. Every table row carries
a \fBSource\fR column with the same word. Updates are classified into
one of three buckets:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B Ready to install
\(em hold window has expired; safe to upgrade
.IP \(bu 2
.B Held
\(em still within the tier's hold window, the newest build still inside its
safety wait, or Tier 1 package with
.B manual_signoff = true
.IP \(bu 2
.B Unknown
\(em no usable build date (locally built, disabled repo, helper lookup
failed, or a sync-DB entry that doesn't match the pending candidate's version)
.RE
Each
.B Unknown
package triggers an interactive
.B "[y/N]"
prompt. Pending official repo upgrades come from
.BR checkupdates (8);
pending AUR upgrades come from
.B "<helper> -Qua" .
Build dates come from the fresh sync-database snapshot
.BR checkupdates (8)
itself just synced (its private dbpath: \fB$CHECKUPDATES_DB\fR, default
\fB${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/checkup-db-<uid>/\fR) \(em the same snapshot that produced
the candidate list \(em and, for AUR packages not found in any sync DB, from
the helper's cached metadata (\fB<helper> -Sai\fR). If the snapshot is
missing, nog falls back to \fB/var/lib/pacman/sync\fR with a warning; holds
may then be dated from stale build dates. A hold is only ever dated from a DB
entry matching the pending candidate's exact version \(em mismatches bucket
as Unknown.
The handoff runs in four steps, skipping any source with nothing cleared.
.B pacman -Syu
upgrades the official repositories with
.B --ignore=
listing all held and user-skipped packages; the helper is then invoked as
.B "<helper> -S"
with the cleared AUR package names only, so a held AUR package is never named
and cannot move. Flatpak and snap follow, unchanged.
.PP
Failure handling differs by step. If
.B pacman
fails, nog cancels without touching another source \(em AUR packages are
compiled against official libraries and must not be built on a system whose
repository upgrade did not complete. If a later step fails, nog reports it and
asks whether to continue, defaulting to
.BR no ;
a non-interactive run stops.
.PP
Every run is appended to a per-day CSV log
.RB ( "YYYYMMDD nog-update.csv"
under
.BR "[paths] run_logs" ,
default
.IR ~/.local/share/nog/logs )
with one row per pending package. Since v1.4.3 each row records its own
.B source
.RB ( pacman ,
.BR aur ,
.BR flatpak ,
.BR snap )
and its own
.B outcome
\(em
.B held
for a package nog held,
.B skipped
for an unknown you declined, and otherwise the result of that package's own
source step:
.BR installed ,
.BR "did not complete (status N)" ,
.B cancelled
(you said no at nog's own prompt) or
.B not run
(an earlier step stopped the run). When a step does not complete, the
.B detail
column keeps the line that explains it: the tool's last
.B error:
line, or the question that was declined. The tool's own output still reaches
the terminal live; nog keeps a copy of the end of its error stream, nothing
more. Logs older than 90 days are pruned automatically. Logging soft-fails with
a warning and never blocks an update.
.PP
Before the buckets render,
.B nog update
checks installed kernel and
.IR <kernel>-headers
versions for every Tier 1 kernel. If any pair disagrees
(e.g.
.IR linux-lts
at 6.18.22-1 but
.IR linux-lts-headers
at 6.18.29-1, the fingerprint of a v1.0.2-style desync), a red warning block
is printed naming each desynced pair and pointing at
.BR --realign .
.TP
.B nog update \-\-realign
Recover from a kernel / headers version mismatch. For each desynced kernel
whose pending upgrade version matches the installed headers version, the row
is pulled from the
.B Held
bucket into the
.B Ready
bucket with the annotation
.RB [ "Tier 1 · realigned to match installed headers" ].
The subsequent transaction upgrades the kernel to match the headers in a
single coherent step, after which the next DKMS rebuild succeeds. If no held
kernel matches the installed headers version, a clean fall-through notice is
printed and
.B nog update
proceeds with the standard plan.
.TP
.B nog search \fIquery\fR
Search for packages matching
.IR query
via
.BR pacman (8).
Each result is annotated by tier using the Catppuccin Mocha palette
(red Tier 1, yellow Tier 2, green Tier 3).
.TP
.B nog pin \fIpackage\fR \fB--tier=\fR\fI<1|2|3>\fR
Pin a package to a specific tier. The change is written to
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
via
.B sudo tee
and survives reboots. Pinning to Tier 3 removes any existing Tier 1 or Tier 2
entry for that package (Tier 3 is the implicit default for anything not listed).
.TP
.B nog unlock \fIpackage\fR [\fB--promote\fR]
Without
.BR --promote ,
this command is informational only. Tier 1 packages under
.B manual_signoff = true
are held wholesale; there is no per-session unlock state to toggle, so this
mode simply prints the held status and points at
.BR --promote .
With
.BR --promote ,
force-upgrade the package immediately via
.BR pacman (8)
or the configured helper, bypassing the hold window and any sign-off policy.
This is the escape hatch for a held Tier 1 package.
.TP
.B nog deactivate \fIsource\fR
Source kill switch (v1.0.9; \fBflatpak\fR v1.1.0, \fBsnap\fR v1.2.0). Valid
sources:
.BR aur ,
.BR chaotic-aur ,
.BR flatpak ,
and
.BR snap .
Deactivating
.B flatpak
persists a flag in
.I /etc/nog/sources.toml
so
.B nog update
neither queries nor applies flatpak updates; installed apps stay installed
but frozen. The flatpak binary itself is never touched.
Deactivating
.B aur
persists a flag in
.I /etc/nog/sources.toml
and turns off every AUR-aware path at once — update detection, install
routing, and the upgrade handoff (which then runs through
.BR pacman (8)
only, so foreign packages cannot move). The configured helper in
.I nog.conf
is left untouched and resumes on activation. Deactivating
.B chaotic-aur
comments the \fB[chaotic-aur]\fR section out of
.I /etc/pacman.conf
(timestamped backup first, marker \fB#nog#\fR), then refreshes the sync
databases: no tool on the system can resolve from the repository until it is
reactivated, and installed chaotic packages stay installed but frozen.
Intended for incident response — e.g. an active AUR supply-chain attack.
.TP
.B nog activate \fIsource\fR
Reverse of
.BR "nog deactivate" .
Restores the AUR paths, or uncomments exactly the pacman.conf lines nog
disabled (hand-written comments survive byte-for-byte) and refreshes the
databases.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help information.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version information.
.
.SH FLATPAK SUPPORT
Since v1.1.0
.B nog update
also reports and applies Flatpak app updates, under the same tier hold
windows as everything else. The hold clock for a flatpak ref is the
publish date of the pending remote commit (from
.BR "flatpak remote-info" ),
the analogue of a package's build date. Flatpak refs classify as Tier 3 by
default and can be pinned like any package with
.BR "nog pin" .
Rows carry a
.B "\(bu flatpak"
marker in the Note column so the source of every update is visible before
you confirm.
.PP
Flatpak is an optional backend: if the
.B flatpak
binary is absent the source is simply dormant, never an error. Because
flatpak has no
.B \-\-ignore
option, nog enforces holds by naming: it passes exactly the refs it cleared
this run and nothing else. A failed remote query is reported and the run
continues with flatpak untouched \(em never assumed quiet.
.
.SH SNAP SUPPORT
Since v1.2.0
.B nog update
also reports and refreshes Snap packages, under the same tier hold windows.
The hold clock for a snap is the publish date of the pending revision in the
snap's tracked channel, read from
.BR "snap info" .
Rows carry a
.B "\(bu snap"
marker in the Note column.
.PP
snapd is packaged only in the AUR on Arch, so nog never requires it: without
the
.B snap
binary the source is dormant and silent, never an error. Unlike every other
nog operation,
.B "snap refresh"
requires root \(em nog stays unprivileged and escalates through
.BR sudo (8)
for the snap steps alone.
.PP
Snap holds are enforced differently from flatpak, and the difference matters.
snapd runs its own auto\-refresh on its own schedule \(em four times a day by
default \(em so naming only the cleared snaps at refresh time held nothing:
snapd refreshed the rest regardless. Since v1.4.1 nog places the tier window
in snapd itself, with
.BR "snap refresh --hold=<hours>" ,
where it actually binds. A held snap is protected from snapd's timer and from
a blanket
.BR "snap refresh" ,
while a specifically named refresh stays unblocked \(em which is how nog still
releases the snap itself once the window closes. If a hold cannot be placed,
nog says
.B "NOT HELD"
rather than reporting a hold it did not achieve.
.
.SH REBOOT ADVICE
After a successful update,
.B nog
compares the running system against what is now installed and, if they differ,
ends the run with a recommendation. It never reboots or restarts anything.
.PP
Every line states its own basis, and the distinction is deliberate:
.TP
.B verified
.B nog
looked at the running system and observed the difference. The line carries both
versions. Three checks are available:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the running kernel, by whether
.I /usr/lib/modules/<uname \-r>
still exists \(em that directory is named for the running kernel and is removed
when the kernel is replaced, so no version string is ever parsed
.IP \(bu 2
the loaded NVIDIA driver, from
.I /proc/driver/nvidia/version
.IP \(bu 2
the running init system, from
.B systemctl \-\-version
.RE
.TP
.B advice
.B nog
confirmed the package was installed but has no way to ask what is running \(em
.BR glibc ,
.B mkinitcpio
and
.B grub
have no such probe. The packages are named and the notice says plainly that it
is advice rather than a finding.
.PP
.BR mesa ,
.BR xorg\-server ,
.B wayland
and
.B dbus
produce a log-out notice instead of a reboot recommendation.
.I <X>\-headers
packages produce nothing: they are never loaded into a running system and they
accompany every kernel.
.PP
Since v1.4.3 the advice is also written down, to
.B YYYYMMDD nog-reboot.csv
beside the run logs: one row per line of advice with its level
.RB ( IMPORTANT ,
.BR NOTE ),
or a single
.B checked
row when the probes ran and found nothing to say. It is written after the run
log is already on disk, so a probe problem can never cost the main record.
.PP
Nothing is printed when there is nothing to report. A package
.B nog
cleared but pacman did not install produces no notice \(em installed versions are
re-read after the handoff rather than assumed from the request \(em and a driver
whose loaded module already matches is silent.
.PP
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/nog/nog.conf
Main nog configuration file. Defines paths, hold windows (\fB[holds]\fR), and
AUR helper preference (\fB[aur]\fR).
.TP
.I /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
Tier assignments for all managed packages. Edited by
.B nog pin
(written via
.BR "sudo tee" )
or manually by the system administrator.
.B manual_signoff
under
.B [tier1]
controls whether Tier 1 auto-releases (default) or stays wholesale held
(expert mode).
.TP
.I /var/lib/pacman/sync/*.db
Pacman sync databases. Read-only by nog, for build-date lookup during
.BR "nog update" .
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/nog/holds.tsv
The hold record (v1.5.2): one line per pending update \(em source, name,
installed version, first sighting (Unix time) and every new version seen since.
Written by
.B nog update
as the invoking user; path set by \fBhold_record\fR under \fB[paths]\fR. Holds
that began before the record existed are dated from the run logs. Deleting it is
safe: holds fall back to the pre-v1.5.2 rule until it is rebuilt.
.TP
.I /etc/nog/sources.toml
Source kill-switch state, managed by
.B nog activate
/
.BR "nog deactivate" ;
written via
.BR "sudo tee" .
A missing file or missing key means the source is active. An unreadable file
fails CLOSED — every source is treated as deactivated until the file is
rewritten by an activate/deactivate command.
.TP
.I /etc/pacman.conf
Parsed for enabled repositories. Written only by
.B nog activate/deactivate chaotic-aur
(section toggle with a \fB#nog#\fR marker), always preceded by a timestamped
backup (\fIpacman.conf.nog-bak-<stamp>\fR).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/nog/logs/
Per-run CSV update logs (\fBYYYYMMDD nog-update.csv\fR), one row per pending
package per run with its source, its own outcome and, when a step did not
complete, the reason; plus reboot advice (\fBYYYYMMDD nog-reboot.csv\fR); and,
since v1.5.8, one line per nog run of any command
(\fBYYYYMMDD nog-runs.csv\fR: date, time, user, command, status, outcome).
Every run ends by naming the logs it wrote.
Columns since v1.4.3:
.nf
.B "  date,time,user,source,bucket,package,"
.B "  old_version,new_version,tier,note,outcome,detail"
.fi
Earlier files keep their original ten-column header; on the day of the upgrade
the new header follows a blank line in the same file. Location configurable via
.B [paths] run_logs
in
.IR nog.conf ;
files older than 90 days are pruned after each run.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/nog
The nog binary.
.
.SH TIER SYSTEM
Every package nog manages is classified into one of three tiers, defined in
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml .
Each tier has a hold window (from the
.B [holds]
section of
.IR /etc/nog/nog.conf )
that must elapse after the package's upstream publish date before the update is
applied during
.BR "nog update" .
.PP
The keyring packages \fBarchlinux-keyring\fR and \fBchaotic-keyring\fR are
never held (since v1.5.6): holding them back would make every later signature
check fail. They are installed in their own step before the rest of the update.
If pacman's key store was never set up, nog runs \fBpacman-key --init\fR and
\fB--populate\fR first.
.PP
Since v1.5.2 the window is counted from the \fBfirst\fR new version seen for the
installed version, not from the newest build: Arch carries only the newest build,
and a new build every week or two used to restart the countdown indefinitely
(issue #27). First sightings are kept in the hold record (see
.BR FILES ).
When the window is over, the newest version is released once it is itself at
least the tier's \fBsafety wait\fR old: \fBtier1_safety_days\fR (default 7),
\fBtier2_safety_days\fR (3) and \fBtier3_safety_days\fR (1) under
.BR [holds] .
Rows show \fBwaiting since <date> \(pc N newer versions skipped\fR, and
\fBnewest build too new\fR during the safety wait.
.SS Tier 1 \(em 30-day hold, auto-release by default
The most critical packages: kernel, bootloader, glibc, systemd, mesa. By default
(\fBmanual_signoff = false\fR), Tier 1 auto-releases after a 30-day hold window
\(em a full month of community testing before an update lands on your system.
.PP
Expert mode: set
.B manual_signoff = true
under
.B [tier1]
in
.IR tier-pins.toml
to switch Tier 1 to always-held; updates release only via
.B "nog unlock <pkg> --promote" .
Recommended only for users who want to review every kernel / glibc / systemd
upgrade personally.
.PP
Default Tier 1 packages:
.BR linux ", " linux-zen ", " linux-lts ", " linux-hardened ", " systemd ", "
.BR systemd-libs ", " glibc ", " grub ", " efibootmgr ", " mkinitcpio ", "
.BR pacman ", " mesa .
.SS Tier 2 \(em 15-day hold
Key desktop applications and system services. Held for 15 days after upstream
publish \(em enough time for major regressions to surface without falling far behind.
.PP
Default Tier 2 packages:
.BR plasma-meta ", " plasma-desktop ", " sddm ", " pipewire ", " pipewire-pulse ", "
.BR wireplumber ", " networkmanager ", " firefox ", " dolphin ", " konsole ", "
.BR kate ", " grubforge ", " alacritty ", " fish ", " alacrittyforge .
.SS Tier 3 \(em 7-day hold
Everything else. A short 7-day safety buffer without meaningful delay. Anything
not listed in Tier 1 or Tier 2 falls into Tier 3 automatically.
.SS Coupling \(em \fI<X>-headers\fR follows its kernel
A kernel package and its matching
.IR <name>-headers
package share a single PKGBUILD upstream, which means they share a single
build date and must be installed at the same version on disk \(em every DKMS
hook (e.g.
.BR nvidia-open-dkms (1))
rebuilds against
.IR <kernel>-headers
and places its output under
.IR /usr/lib/modules/<KVER>/ ,
which is only present when
.IR <kernel>
itself is installed at the matching version.
.PP
.B nog
auto-couples this relationship: any package matching the
.IR <X>-headers
pattern is classified as Tier 1 when
.IR <X>
is Tier 1. This is hardcoded behavior, not configurable. The Arch naming
convention is universal and the failure mode (broken DKMS module, unbound
GPU after reboot) is severe enough that opting out is not supported.
.SS Groups (advanced)
The optional
.B [groups]
table in
.IR /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml
is the escape hatch for non-standard kernel names whose headers do not match
the
.IR <X>-headers
pattern (e.g.
.IR linux-cachyos-cacule-headers ),
or for bundling extra packages into a kernel's tier.
.PP
.RS
.nf
[groups]
cachyos-bundle = [
    "linux-cachyos",
    "linux-cachyos-headers",
    "linux-cachyos-cacule-headers",
]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every member of a group inherits the highest tier present among any other
member of the same group.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Install a package (nog prompts for sudo itself if needed):
.B nog install htop
.TP
Update the system:
.B nog update
.TP
Search with tier annotations:
.B nog search firefox
.TP
Pin a package to Tier 2:
.B nog pin htop --tier=2
.TP
Force-upgrade a held Tier 1 package:
.B nog unlock linux-zen --promote
.
.SH PRIVILEGES AND SUDO
.B nog
itself always runs as the invoking user; it is not designed to be run with
.BR sudo .
If you prefix
.B sudo nog
while an AUR helper is configured, nog detects the invocation (via
.B $SUDO_USER
or
.BR $SUDO_UID )
and exits with an error, because
.BR yay (8)
and
.BR paru (8)
refuse to run as root.
.PP
nog escalates to root in exactly five places, all as transparent
.BR sudo (8)
subprocess invocations:
.TP
.B sudo pacman -S | -U | -R | -Syu ...
For package transactions.
.B "nog update"
always hands the official repositories to
.B "sudo pacman -Syu"
directly (since v1.3.0, one manager per source), and
.B "nog install"
of a package file always uses
.B "sudo pacman -U"
(v1.5.0).
.BR install " by name, " remove " and " "unlock --promote"
use
.B sudo pacman
only when no AUR helper is configured; with a helper, nog hands the
transaction to the helper as the invoking user, and the helper runs its own
.B sudo pacman
internally.
.TP
.B "sudo rm -f -- <cache files> | sudo rm -rf -- <cache>/download-XXXXXX"
Only in
.B "nog clean"
(v1.5.5), only after you answer yes, and only on package files and stale
leftover download folders directly inside pacman's configured cache directories.
.TP
.B "sudo snap refresh --hold=<hours> | sudo snap refresh <names>"
For snap holds and snap refreshes (v1.2.0; the hold step added in v1.4.1).
snapd requires root for both. Nothing else about the snap source does.
.TP
.B "sudo tee /etc/nog/tier-pins.toml | /etc/nog/sources.toml"
For the files
.B nog
writes, during
.BR "nog pin" " and " "nog activate" / "nog deactivate" .
The new contents are rebuilt in memory and piped through
.BR tee ;
nog itself never runs with root privileges.
.TP
.B sudo cp --preserve=all /etc/pacman.conf ...
To take a timestamped backup before editing the Chaotic\-AUR section, and only
during
.BR "nog activate" / "nog deactivate chaotic-aur" .
.PP
Outside that one Chaotic\-AUR section of
.IR /etc/pacman.conf ,
.B nog
modifies no other file on your system \(em not
.IR /etc/pacman.d/ ,
not
.IR /var/lib/pacman/local/ ,
not the pacman keyring, and not
.IR /etc/sudoers .
It is a thin wrapper over
.BR pacman (8),
.BR yay (8),
or
.BR paru (8);
every install, remove, and upgrade is subject to pacman's signature
verification and conflict resolution, which nog cannot bypass.
.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS \fBcheckupdates failed: Cannot find the fakeroot binary\fR
The update check comes from \fBpacman-contrib\fR and needs \fBfakeroot\fR, which
pacman-contrib lists only as optional. nog depends on it since v1.5.7; on an older
install, or if it was removed, install it together with a full update:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo pacman -Syu fakeroot
.RE
.PP
Not \fBpacman -S fakeroot\fR alone: on old package lists that is a partial upgrade.
.SS DKMS rebuild fails after \fBnog update\fR
If a DKMS hook (typically
.BR nvidia-open-dkms ,
.BR nvidia-dkms ,
or
.BR virtualbox-host-dkms )
emits
.RS
.PP
.B "ERROR: Missing <KVER> kernel modules tree for module <name>/<version>."
.RE
.PP
your kernel and its headers have diverged on disk \(em headers are at
.IR <KVER>
but the kernel binary for that version isn't installed under
.IR /usr/lib/modules/<KVER>/ .
.PP
In v1.0.3 and later this state is detected automatically; the desync warning
block in
.B nog update
points at the recovery flag:
.PP
.RS
.B nog update --realign
.RE
.PP
Manual recovery (use when
.B --realign
cannot apply, e.g. headers are ahead of any pending kernel upgrade):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo pacman -S linux-zen linux-lts      # adjust to your kernels
sudo pacman -S nvidia-open-dkms          # or the failing DKMS package
.fi
.RE
.PP
Verify the rebuild with
.B dkms status
and look for the expected
.I .ko.zst
files under
.IR /usr/lib/modules/$(uname\ -r)/updates/dkms/ .
.SS Verifying coupling is active
.B nog search linux-zen-headers
should annotate the result with
.RB [ "Tier 1 \(em 30d hold" ].
A green Tier 3 annotation means you are still running v1.0.2 or earlier;
upgrade nog before the next
.BR "nog update" .
.
.SS \fBinstalling <A> breaks dependency '<lib>.so=N' required by <B>\fR
pacman refuses the whole transaction and nothing installs. A package whose hold
has expired bumps a shared library version, while a package still linking the
old one is inside its window; nog cleared one and held the other, and pacman
cannot split the pair.
.PP
Nothing is broken \(em pacman caught it and declined. Move the pair forward
together with
.BR "nog install <A> <B>" ,
which installs both in one transaction. Never downgrade the cleared package
back; the safe direction is forward, onto the version the repositories already
carry. Waiting also works: the held package releases on its own schedule and
the wall disappears with it.
.PP
As of v1.3.1
.B nog
holds the pair rather than letting you reach this: the row that would have
been Ready moves to Held and names its partner, e.g.
.RB \(dq "1 day \(bu coupled to ffmpeg4.4" \(dq,
and both clear on the same run. When the partner has no pending update at all
\(em a foreign or AUR package built against the old library \(em there is no
countdown to inherit and the note reads
.RB \(dq "blocked by <package>" \(dq,
meaning the hold will not lift on its own: rebuild or update that package, or
move the pair forward with
.B nog install
as above.
.PP
Since v1.4.2 the rule also reads the programs themselves. A package can link
a library without declaring it at the right version \(em chaotic-aur's
.B ffmpeg-obs
declared plain
.B libbluray
while linking
.BR libbluray.so.3 ,
so when
.B libbluray
moved to
.B .so.4
pacman was satisfied and nine packages broke silently. When a pending update
drops a library version,
.B nog
now reads the
.B DT_NEEDED
entries of every installed program and library and treats a real linker
exactly like a declared one. The scan runs only when an update drops a library
version, which is rare, and prints a line saying so. A package that ships its
own copy of the library is not counted as a linker, and a package that ships
the library file on the loader path keeps it available even without declaring
it.
.PP
The raw pacman error is still reachable on v1.3.0 and earlier, or when the
local package database at
.I /var/lib/pacman/local
cannot be read \(em
.B nog
says so and carries on without the rule.
.SS pacman's log shows only one ignored package
.B nog
passes a single comma-joined
.B \-\-ignore
argument. pacman splits that string in place inside its own
.IR argv ,
then writes its
.RB \(dq Running\ '...' \(dq
line, so the log records the first name and drops the rest \(em one name can
mean a hundred and sixty were passed. The holds were applied correctly; the
.RB \(dq "warning: <pkg>: ignoring package upgrade" \(dq
lines are the accurate record. A pacman logging artefact, not a nog defect.
